CFItems.notes:
kind: overgeneralization. status: draft. Evidence: on 'predict a write one past the end of an array', predicts the array now has one more element. Example: int[] a = new int[3];
a[3] = 7;
System.out.println(a.length); -> 4 (expected ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException).

kind: undergeneralization. status: draft. Evidence: on 'predict whether a guarded expression raises an error', predicts an error from the second operand even though the first operand decides the result. Example: items = []
if len(items) > 0 and items[0] == 'x':
    print('yes')
else:
    print('no') -> IndexError (expected no).

kind: overgeneralization. status: draft. Evidence: on 'trace an if-else where the condition is true', reports the effects of both branches. Example: x = 5
if x > 0:
    print('positive')
else:
    print('not positive') -> positive
not positive (expected positive).

kind: overgeneralization. status: draft. Evidence: on 'predict a mutation through a final/const reference', predicts an error. Example: const items = [1, 2];
items.push(3);
console.log(items); -> TypeError (or 'not allowed') (expected [1, 2, 3]).

kind: overgeneralization. status: draft. Evidence: on 'predict == on two distinct objects with equal contents', predicts true. Example: String a = new String("hi");
String b = new String("hi");
System.out.println(a == b); -> true (expected false).

kind: overgeneralization. status: draft. Evidence: on 'predict the outcome of reading a declared but unassigned local variable', predicts 0 or empty output rather than a compile error or garbage value. Example: int count;
count = count + 1;
System.out.println(count); -> 1 (expected Compile error: count might not have been initialized).
